562fe3cc3c29537db797606a2eca81d1a003c06b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/gedu/repack-atlas/commits/562fe3cc3c29537db797606a2eca81d1a003c06b"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/1676818?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/gedu/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/gedu/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/gedu/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/gedu/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/gedu","id":1676818,"login":"gedu","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE2NzY4MTg=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/gedu/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/gedu/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/gedu/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/gedu/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/gedu/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/gedu","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/gedu/repack-atlas/commits/d7fe4bca6a4f6e8d29a155373ee1a444f3d4c480/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-02T13:06:56Z","email":"gedu@users.noreply.github.com","name":"edug"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-02T13:06:56Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(dev): warn on busy ports in the wizard and offer to kill workspace orphans (#60)\n\n* feat(runner): probe the owning process behind a busy port\n\nAnswers \"who holds this port?\" behind a runner-owned port so the dev\ncommand can name the owner instead of only reporting the clash (ODD\ndev-port-conflict-warn-kill T1). PortOwnerProbe/ProcessTerminator join\nPortProbe without extending it: owner lookup legitimately degrades to\nnull (Windows, no lsof, another user''s process) and every caller must\ntreat that as \"busy, owner unknown\", never a guess. The node adapter\nspawns lsof then ps through the SAME ProcessRunner (wrapper rule 3, no\nnew dependencies) and createNodeProcessRunner composes the capability,\nreturning the honest degradation shapes one by one: no lsof listener,\nno LISTEN row, a vanished pid, or a runner that cannot spawn at all all\nanswer null. terminate refuses pid <= 1 before signalling — 0/-1 would\nhit our own process group — and reports ESRCH/EPERM as false so a\nkill that did not land falls through to the old error path.\n\nallocatePorts gains the additive busyPorts on a failed allocation (the\nbusy declared ports only, in plan order: the subset a kill could ever\nfix, absent when the conflict is duplicate-declared-only) and\nformatOwnerCommand caps one owner command line for a question. The two\nexisting deep-equal conflict tests carry the new field; busyPorts''\npresence contract (absent-but-ok, absent-not-busy, present-on-throwing\n-probe, absent under --auto-ports) gets its own suite, plus the parser\ntests over canned lsof/ps output and one real-listener check that\nproves the canned shapes match the actual tools.\n\n* feat(cli): name the busy default inside the wizard port question\n\nToday a busy port surfaces only after the LAST wizard question, when the\nallocator runs — a seven-question dead end (ODD dev-port-conflict-warn-\nkill A, from a real incident where four orphans held 8081/9001-9003 for\n9h+). WizardContext takes an optional busy/owner probe; a busy declared\ndefault says so in its own question, naming the owner when the OS can:\n`Use port 8081 for host? (in use: node …/react-native/cli.js start)`,\ncapped through formatOwnerCommand. Keeping a busy answer stays allowed —\nthe allocator still decides, --auto-ports may move it.\n\nThe absences are the contract: context without a probe (every machine\npath and every caller predating this) asks byte-identical questions, a\nfree default adds no suffix, an auto (null-port) app is never probed,\nand a probe that throws counts as busy with the bare \"(in use)\" note —\nit cannot vouch for freeness.\n\n* feat(cli): offer to kill orphaned dev servers on a port conflict\n\nA hard parent death (client crash) leaves a spawned react-native start\nparentless, holding its port, and the next dev run dead-ends on the\nconflict (ODD dev-port-conflict-warn-kill C, the 9h+ four-orphan\nincident). On a portConflict in the HUMAN path only, resolve each\nconflicting port''s owner and offer a kill ONLY for an owner that is\nprovably an orphaned dev server of THIS workspace: PPID 1 AND an app\ndir of the resolved config in its argv (isWorkspaceOrphan). Never a\nblind \"kill whatever holds the port\" — an unknown owner, a live\nprocess, a foreign argv, or one non-orphan among several all mean no\nprompt at all (all-or-nothing), and the honesty rule holds: the \"of\nthis workspace\" claim is made only when the argv actually matched.\n\nofferOrphanKills asks one confirm per port through PromptPort (the same\nink/clack/readline routing as the wizard), SIGTERMs the confirmed pids\nthrough the ProcessTerminator port, and waits a bounded time for each\nport to free; ''killed'' only when ALL freed, after which dev.ts retries\nthe plan exactly ONCE and reports each kill. Every other shape —\nunavailable, declined, terminate false, a port that stayed busy — keeps\ntoday''s error path byte for byte: same conflict lines, same exit 1.\n--json/--ci/non-TTY never probe an owner, never prompt, never signal\n(the gate demands both TTY flags plus the absence of both flags, and\nresolveDevPlan only carries busyPorts/appDirs when busy declared ports\nactually caused the conflict).\n\nTests: the matcher, the question/cap wording, and every outcome branch\nover fakes (no real pids or signals); the conflict shape resolveDevPlan\nfeeds it over real adapters; and the dev.ts seam end to end — two\nconfirmed orphans killed into a live supervised session with the notes\nshown, a decline landing on the exact old lines, and --json/--ci\nbyte-identical with ownerAsks/terminated provably empty. A mutation\ncheck (gate forced false) fails exactly the human-path cases.\n\n* docs(odd): record port-conflict feature closure and review follow-ups","tree":{"sha":"b875eb3a3761f8115ee6febba12dd7a856a9aa76","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/gedu/repack-atlas/git/trees/b875eb3a3761f8115ee6febba12dd7a856a9aa76"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/gedu/repack-atlas/git/commits/d7fe4bca6a4f6e8d29a155373ee1a444f3d4c480","verification":{"payload":"tree b875eb3a3761f8115ee6febba12dd7a856a9aa76\nparent 8f0c340bd9d011aa2cc1b0a0d6cc110fb8b15261\nauthor edug <gedu@users.noreply.github.com> 1790946416 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790946416 +0200\n\nfix(dev): warn on busy ports in the wizard and offer to kill workspace orphans (#60)\n\n* feat(runner): probe the owning process behind a busy port\n\nAnswers \"who holds this port?\" behind a runner-owned port so the dev\ncommand can name the owner instead of only reporting the clash (ODD\ndev-port-conflict-warn-kill T1). PortOwnerProbe/ProcessTerminator join\nPortProbe without extending it: owner lookup legitimately degrades to\nnull (Windows, no lsof, another user''s process) and every caller must\ntreat that as \"busy, owner unknown\", never a guess. The node adapter\nspawns lsof then ps through the SAME ProcessRunner (wrapper rule 3, no\nnew dependencies) and createNodeProcessRunner composes the capability,\nreturning the honest degradation shapes one by one: no lsof listener,\nno LISTEN row, a vanished pid, or a runner that cannot spawn at all all\nanswer null. terminate refuses pid <= 1 before signalling — 0/-1 would\nhit our own process group — and reports ESRCH/EPERM as false so a\nkill that did not land falls through to the old error path.\n\nallocatePorts gains the additive busyPorts on a failed allocation (the\nbusy declared ports only, in plan order: the subset a kill could ever\nfix, absent when the conflict is duplicate-declared-only) and\nformatOwnerCommand caps one owner command line for a question. The two\nexisting deep-equal conflict tests carry the new field; busyPorts''\npresence contract (absent-but-ok, absent-not-busy, present-on-throwing\n-probe, absent under --auto-ports) gets its own suite, plus the parser\ntests over canned lsof/ps output and one real-listener check that\nproves the canned shapes match the actual tools.\n\n* feat(cli): name the busy default inside the wizard port question\n\nToday a busy port surfaces only after the LAST wizard question, when the\nallocator runs — a seven-question dead end (ODD dev-port-conflict-warn-\nkill A, from a real incident where four orphans held 8081/9001-9003 for\n9h+). WizardContext takes an optional busy/owner probe; a busy declared\ndefault says so in its own question, naming the owner when the OS can:\n`Use port 8081 for host? (in use: node …/react-native/cli.js start)`,\ncapped through formatOwnerCommand. Keeping a busy answer stays allowed —\nthe allocator still decides, --auto-ports may move it.\n\nThe absences are the contract: context without a probe (every machine\npath and every caller predating this) asks byte-identical questions, a\nfree default adds no suffix, an auto (null-port) app is never probed,\nand a probe that throws counts as busy with the bare \"(in use)\" note —\nit cannot vouch for freeness.\n\n* feat(cli): offer to kill orphaned dev servers on a port conflict\n\nA hard parent death (client crash) leaves a spawned react-native start\nparentless, holding its port, and the next dev run dead-ends on the\nconflict (ODD dev-port-conflict-warn-kill C, the 9h+ four-orphan\nincident). On a portConflict in the HUMAN path only, resolve each\nconflicting port''s owner and offer a kill ONLY for an owner that is\nprovably an orphaned dev server of THIS workspace: PPID 1 AND an app\ndir of the resolved config in its argv (isWorkspaceOrphan). Never a\nblind \"kill whatever holds the port\" — an unknown owner, a live\nprocess, a foreign argv, or one non-orphan among several all mean no\nprompt at all (all-or-nothing), and the honesty rule holds: the \"of\nthis workspace\" claim is made only when the argv actually matched.\n\nofferOrphanKills asks one confirm per port through PromptPort (the same\nink/clack/readline routing as the wizard), SIGTERMs the confirmed pids\nthrough the ProcessTerminator port, and waits a bounded time for each\nport to free; ''killed'' only when ALL freed, after which dev.ts retries\nthe plan exactly ONCE and reports each kill. Every other shape —\nunavailable, declined, terminate false, a port that stayed busy — keeps\ntoday''s error path byte for byte: same conflict lines, same exit 1.\n--json/--ci/non-TTY never probe an owner, never prompt, never signal\n(the gate demands both TTY flags plus the absence of both flags, and\nresolveDevPlan only carries busyPorts/appDirs when busy declared ports\nactually caused the conflict).\n\nTests: the matcher, the question/cap wording, and every outcome branch\nover fakes (no real pids or signals); the conflict shape resolveDevPlan\nfeeds it over real adapters; and the dev.ts seam end to end — two\nconfirmed orphans killed into a live supervised session with the notes\nshown, a decline landing on the exact old lines, and --json/--ci\nbyte-identical with ownerAsks/terminated provably empty. A mutation\ncheck (gate forced false) fails exactly the human-path cases.\n\n* docs(odd): record port-conflict feature closure and review follow-ups","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqv6xwCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAZxgQAK+PE8pQiv1RQz7M/nBRaVzc\nDkzSk7NTCFTIeDkR+ep4vWsQn1qHkY1dOZeZCiY8drUDHDuBEb36ZJaFdR2Xk+vE\n0QKsQEg2MG6Uz98YbFnDhPhgz+BQYE90H8eDZvL2AmCC+9QovUeCs5fqs68BknO1\nIdeOnaCZmeZ0dR5TOKVDnj7Z089JKfCCSqySa60yqS0TEi1/ZLNykW0E0di41yE8\n9p6RtxzGFFA/VH5M2FrtwAbG9Y2nCdLgIVt8AJ1zT+S12bbecPYkkTMVtnNsJ4nt\nB5/CVnEMeiYTZqE5+0c1JCB3YwvL73NBVad4lud+b0XYM8sj1DUJykO+Hpy7lxR7\n6oLXuAZso5dODdPURfTEEXYaOdqwHl2+YZ7U6qM/+oRHrLJjb9gLjOkAOECTi3V4\nm5icwqV1npNvZqxhjXrtgwS3g/ZYNZ5f